{
  "dom_id": "step-q74273",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=16{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $1?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $16{x}^{3}$ and $-4{x}^{2}$ is $4{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =16{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}(4x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor ${x}^{2}$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(4x-1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ has multiplicity $1.$",
